'''''Thymelaea passerina''''', the '''spurge flax''', '''sparrow weed''', '''mezereon''', or '''annual thymelaea''', is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Thymelaeaceae]].<ref name="FNA_Tp" >{{cite web |url=http://floranorthamerica.org/Thymelaea_passerina |title=''Thymelaea passerina'' (Linnaeus) Cosson & Germain |last1=Nevling Jr. |first1=Lorin I. |last2=Barringer |first2=Kerry |date=5 November 2020 |website=Flora of North America |publisher=efloras.org |access-date=7 March 2024 |archive-date=13 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210413222045/http://floranorthamerica.org/Thymelaea_passerina |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://data.canadensys.net/vascan/taxon/9414 |title=''Thymelaea passerina'' |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=7 March 2024 |website=canadensys.net |publisher=Database of Vascular Plants of Canada (VASCAN) |access-date=7 March 2024 |archive-date=6 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220506222256/https://data.canadensys.net/vascan/taxon/9414 |url-status=live }}</ref> It is native to central and southern Europe, the Middle East, Central Asia, the western Himalayas, and [[Xinjiang]] in China, and it has been introduced to central North America, and to [[South Australia]].<ref name="POWO_77085178-1" >{{cite POWO |id=77085178-1 |title=''Thymelaea passerina'' (L.) Coss. & Germ. |access-date=7 March 2024 }}</ref> An erect annual from {{cvt|2.5|to|24|in|cm|0|order=flip}} tall, it is typically found in steppes, disturbed areas, and old fields.<ref name="FNA_Tp" />
